## Git Credentials

### GitHub Token

**For HTTPS git operations:**

```bash
# Generate token on GitHub:
# Settings → Developer settings → Personal access tokens → Generate new token

# Store in git credential helper (macOS Keychain)
git config --global credential.helper osxkeychain

# Next time you push, enter:
# Username: your-username
# Password: ghp_yourtoken (NOT your GitHub password!)

# Credentials stored in Keychain
```

### SSH (Recommended)

```bash
# Use SSH instead of HTTPS
# Already configured if you set up SSH keys

# Clone with SSH
git clone git@github.com:username/repo.git

# Or change existing repo
g remote set-url origin git@github.com:username/repo.git
```

### Git Credential Store

```bash
# Store credentials (less secure than keychain)
git config --global credential.helper store

# Credentials stored in ~/.git-credentials (plaintext!)
# NOT recommended
```

---

## Environment Variables

### Session Variables

**For non-secret env vars:**

**File:** `home/jimmy/shell/zsh.nix`

```nix
home.sessionVariables = {
  # Public variables (OK to commit)
  EDITOR = "code --wait";
  MACHINE_MODE = "home";
  AWS_REGION = "us-east-1";
};
```

**For secrets:**

**File:** `~/.zsh_secrets` (NOT in git)

```bash
# Secret variables
export SECRET_KEY="..."
export API_KEY="..."
```

### Project Environment

**Use `.env` files:**

```bash
# In project
cd ~/Dev/my-api

# Create .env
cat > .env <<EOF
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/mydb
SECRET_KEY=secret123
DEBUG=true
EOF

# Add to .gitignore
echo ".env" >> .gitignore

# Create .env.example (safe to commit)
cat > .env.example <<EOF
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://localhost/mydb
SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key-here
DEBUG=false
EOF

# Commit example
g aa .env.example
g cm "Add .env.example"
```

